As used in this article, the term "fireworks" shall not include the following:
A. Model rockets and model rocket engines, designed, sold and used for the purpose of propelling recoverable aero models;
B. Toy pistol paper caps in which the explosive content does not average more than twenty-five hundredths (0.25) grains of explosive mixture per paper cap;
C. Ammunition consumed by weapons used for sporting and hunting purposes;
D. Toy pistols, toy cannons, toy canes, toy guns or other devices using such paper caps;
E. "Sparklers" as defined in Section 4.08.060 of this article.
(Ord. 06-145 § 3, 2006; Ord. 05-124 § 3 (part), 2005)

352:
Nothing in this law shall be construed to prohibit any of the following:
1. The sale, at wholesale, of any fireworks for supervised displays by any approved resident manufacturer, wholesaler, dealer or jobber, in accordance with regulations of the U.S. Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco and Firearms (see Title 27, Code of Federal Regulations, Part 181) and the U.S. Department of Transportation;
2. The manufacture, transportation or storage of fireworks at a manufacturing facility. The testing of fireworks under the direction of its manufacturer provided permission for such testing has been obtained from the authority having jurisdiction;
3. The sale, transportation, handling or use of industrial pyrotechnic devices or fireworks, such as railroad torpedoes, fuses, automotive, aeronautical and marine flares and smoke signals;
4. The sale and use of blank cartridges for use in a show or theater, for signal or ceremonial purposes, in athletics or sports or legal power tools;
5. The transportation, handling or use of any pyrotechnic devices by the armed forces of the United States;
6. The use of pyrotechnics in training by the fire service, law enforcement or similar government agencies;
7. The use of fireworks for agricultural purposes under the direct supervision of the U.S. Department of the Interior or an equivalent state or local agency. [See Title 16, Code of Federal Regulations, Part 1500.17 (a) (8).]
(Ord. 05-124 § 3 (part), 2005)

364:
A. In the event of man-made or natural disaster, mass electrical failure, rioting, actual enemy attack upon the United States or any other emergency which may affect lives and property, the mayor may declare that a state of emergency exists by written order setting out the circumstances of the emergency and that portion of the city affected. Thereafter the mayor, or a designee thereof, shall have and may exercise for any period as this state of emergency exists or continues, the following emergency powers:
1. To enforce all rules, laws and regulations relating to emergency management and to assume direct operational control over all civil defense resources;
2. To seize, take for temporary use, or condemn any property for the protection of the public;
3. To sell, lend, give or distribute all or any property or supplies among the inhabitants of the city; to maintain a strict accounting of property or supplies distributed and for funds received for the property or supplies;
4. To declare a limited or general curfew as may be needed to restore public order;
5. To prohibit or regulate the possession, sale or use of explosives, gasoline or other flammable liquids, firearms, ammunition, or dangerous weapons of any kind;
6. To close any business establishments necessary;
7. To perform and exercise any other functions and duties and take any emergency actions as may be necessary to promote and secure the safety, protection and well-being of the inhabitants of the city.
B. Upon a declaration of an emergency above, the mayor and council shall immediately be called into session to perform any duties necessary.
C. The state of emergency and the resulting powers may be ended immediately upon a motion thereon being adopted by the mayor and council or by an order of the officer declaring the emergency over.
D. Otherwise, such emergency and the powers under this section shall lapse within seventy-two (72) hours of the declaration unless another specific time period is provided in the declaration.
(Added during 1991 codification)

374:
Breeding sources for mosquitoes shall be eliminated or treated by one or more of the following methods as shall be approved by the director.
A. Draining, removing or otherwise eliminating the breeding source;
B. Completely emptying the breeding source of all water at least every seven (7) days, or as approved by the director. Where an artificial container is a water basin (e.g. swimming pool) designed to hold water, such containers shall be maintained or altered to prevent mosquito breeding;
C. Treating the breeding source with an effective insecticide and/or larvicide approved by the director;
D. Completely emptying artificial containers of all liquid and storing in an enclosed structure which is constructed in such a manner as to prevent the permanent collection of liquid in said containers;
E. Maintain all natural or manmade storm or surface water drain ways in a manner as to prevent the ponding of water sufficient to provide breeding for mosquitoes;
F. Other methods proven to be effective in controlling mosquitoes and as approved by the director.
(Ord. 04-104 (part), 2004)

383:
A. Notwithstanding any other provision of this chapter, the following areas located within the city of Stockbridge shall be exempt from the provisions of Sections 4.20.040 and 4.20.050, if one (1) or more of the following requirements have been met and if the requirements of subsection B of this section have also been met:
1. Private residences, except when used as a licensed family day care, adult day care, or health care facility;
2. Any private room or rooms designated for smoking use only ("smoking room") which are located within privately owned, freestanding buildings, so long as any such smoking room or rooms are enclosed and have an air handling system independent from the main air handling system that serves all other areas of the building and all air within any such smoking room is exhausted directly to the outside of the building by an exhaust fan of sufficient size and no air from such smoking room is recirculated through or infiltrates other parts of the building. This exemption is limited to buildings that do not operate a bar, restaurant or private club therein. No person under the age of eighteen (18) shall be permitted to enter any such smoking room;
3. Hotel and motel rooms that are rented to guests and are designated as smoking rooms; provided, however, that not more than twenty percent (20%) of rooms rented to guests in a hotel or motel may be so designated;
4. Retail tobacco stores, provided that secondhand smoke from such stores does not infiltrate into areas where smoking is prohibited under the provisions of this chapter;
5. Long-term care facilities as defined in Official Code of Georgia Annotated Section 31-8-81(3), as amended;
6. Outdoor areas of places of employment;
7. All workplaces of any manufacturer, importer, or wholesaler of tobacco products, of any tobacco leaf dealer or processor, all tobacco storage facilities, and any other entity set forth in Official Code of Georgia Annotated Section l0-13A-2, as amended;
8. Private and semiprivate rooms in health care facilities licensed under Title 31 of the Official Code of Georgia that are occupied by one (1) or more persons, all of whom have written authorization by their treating physician to smoke;
9. Smoking areas designated by an employer, in an establishment other than a restaurant, bar or private club, which shall meet the following requirements:
a. The smoking area shall be located in a nonwork area where no employee, as part of his or her work responsibilities, shall be required to enter, except such work responsibilities shall not include custodial or maintenance work carried out in the smoking area when it is unoccupied,
b. The smoking area shall have an air handling system that is independent from the main air handling system that serves all other areas of the building and all air within the smoking area shall be exhausted directly to the outside by an exhaust fan of sufficient size and capacity for the smoking area and no air from the smoking area shall be recirculated through or infiltrate other parts of the building, and
c. The smoking area shall be for the use of employees only;
10. Private room or rooms designated for smoking use only ("smoking room") that are located in buildings that allow for the operation of a bar, restaurant or private club therein so long as such smoking room or rooms are enclosed and have an air handling system independent from the main air handling system that serves all other areas of the building and all air within such smoking room is exhausted directly to the outside by an exhaust fan of sufficient size and no air from such smoking room is recirculated through or infiltrates other parts of the building. Any such smoking room cannot be part of the servicing area of a restaurant, bar or private club and cannot be located in any dressing room, restroom, locker room or office area of a building that houses a bar, restaurant or private club. No person under the age of eighteen (18) shall be permitted to enter any such smoking room; and
11. Private offices in private places of employment occupied exclusively by smokers over the age of eighteen (18) and which are not subject to public access in the ordinary course of business, if such private offices are enclosed and have an air handling system independent from the main air handling system that serves all other areas of the building and all air within any such private office is exhausted directly to the outside by an exhaust fan of sufficient size, and no air from such private office is recirculated through or infiltrates other parts of the building.
B. Any area described in subsection A of this section (hereinafter "exempt area") shall not become exempt from the provisions of Sections 4.20.040 and 4.20.050 of this chapter unless and until signs have been conspicuously posted in the manner described below:
1. At every entrance to any exempt area, a sign shall be conspicuously posted indicating that smoking is permitted,
2. At every other entrance to an area that is not an exempt area, a sign indicating that smoking is not permitted shall be conspicuously posted, and
3. Notwithstanding anything to the contrary contained herein, there shall be no duty to post any signs at any entrance of those areas described in subsection (A)(1) of this section.
(Ord. 06-152 § 1, 2006)

